from tnx import *
def E(u,un,*a): EN(10,u,un,*a)
U1="Unit 1: His First Flight, Life, The Tempest"
E(1,U1,"his-first-flight","His First Flight (Liam O'Flaherty)",
 "A young seagull stands alone on a ledge, hungry, afraid to take the leap that every bird must take.",
 "The young seagull is the only one in his family who has not flown. His brothers and sister have already left the ledge and his parents have scolded and threatened him, but he is too frightened of the long drop to the sea. After a day without food he sees his mother holding a piece of fish just out of reach, and in hunger he dives at it.",
 "He falls, then finds his wings and soars. His fear disappears and the whole family flies around him. The story shows that fear is often conquered only by trying and that a push, even a harsh one, can help us find our strength.",
 "Words to learn: ledge, precipice, plateau, plaintively and soar. The author uses detailed description of the bird's feelings so that we sympathise with him.",
 ["ledge","precipice","soar","plaintively","courage"],
 ("The seagull flew easily the first time he tried.",["He was afraid for a day.","Hunger and his mother's offer made him dive."]),
 ["m|Who wrote His First Flight?|Liam O'Flaherty|Ruskin Bond|Mark Twain|Liam O'Flaherty.","m|Why did the young seagull not fly?|He was afraid|He was sick|He was tired|He feared the drop.","t|His mother offered him food at a distance.|T|She held the fish out of reach.","m|What does 'soar' mean?|fly high|fall|crawl|Soaring is rising in flight.","s|What was the bird's mother holding?|fish;a piece of fish|A piece of fish.","t|The family stopped him from flying.|F|They encouraged him."])
E(1,U1,"life-poem","Life (Henry Van Dyke)",
 "How should we walk through the years: afraid of the road or glad to take it?",
 "The poet prays to live life from year to year with a forward face and an unreluctant soul. He does not want to hurry towards the goal or turn away from it, to mourn the past or fear the future. Whether the road goes up or down, over rough or smooth, the journey will be joy for a heart that keeps its courage.",
 "The poem is optimistic. It values friendship, adventure and hope, and keeps the spirit of the quest as one grows old. Life is a journey to be enjoyed, not merely endured.",
 "Notice the rhyme scheme of this sonnet-like poem and words like unreluctant, veils, toll and quest.",
 ["sonnet","unreluctant","quest","optimism","journey"],
 ("The poet wants to avoid the future.",["He does not fear it.","He keeps hope and courage."]),
 ["m|Who wrote Life?|Henry Van Dyke|Frost|Blake|Henry Van Dyke.","m|The poet wants a heart with|courage and hope|anger|laziness|He keeps courage.","t|The poet fears the future.|F|He does not hold back in fear.","m|The poem compares life to a|journey|race|battle|It speaks of the way and the road.","s|What does the poet hope to seek: new ... ?|friendship;friendships|New friendship, high adventure.","t|The tone is cheerful.|T|It is optimistic."])
E(1,U1,"the-tempest","The Tempest (Charles Lamb's retelling of Shakespeare)",
 "On a lonely island, a magician father raises a storm to bring his enemies to him.",
 "Prospero and his daughter Miranda are the only inhabitants of an island. Prospero's books of magic have freed good spirits, such as Ariel, from the witch Sycorax, who had imprisoned them in trees. When Miranda sees a ship wrecked in a storm, she begs her father to have pity on the poor souls.",
 "The storm was Prospero's own doing, to bring to the island the people who had once wronged him. The story moves towards forgiveness and reunion. It is a retelling of Shakespeare's play in simple prose.",
 "Words to learn: tempest, spirits, magic, vessel and distress. Lamb's Tales from Shakespeare put plays into prose for young readers.",
 ["tempest","Prospero","Miranda","spirits","magic"],
 ("Miranda caused the storm.",["Prospero raised it by his art.","Miranda begged him to stop."]),
 ["m|Who are the two main islanders?|Prospero and Miranda|Tom and Aunt Polly|Holmes and Watson|Father and daughter.","m|Who retold the tale?|Charles Lamb|Frost|Satyajit Ray|Charles Lamb.","t|Prospero studied magic.|T|His books chiefly dealt with magic.","m|What does 'tempest' mean?|storm|calm|feast|A violent storm.","s|Who was the witch who imprisoned spirits?|Sycorax|Sycorax.","t|Miranda wanted the ship to be wrecked.|F|She pitied the people."])
U2="Unit 2: The Night the Ghost Got In, The Grumble Family, Zigzag"
E(2,U2,"night-ghost-got-in","The Night the Ghost Got In (James Thurber)",
 "A strange sound downstairs at night leads a whole family into a comic muddle.",
 "The narrator hears something being trodden on in the house on the night of 17 November 1915. He thinks it is his father, his brother Roy or a burglar, and only later thinks of a ghost. The false alarm causes a hullabaloo of misunderstandings among the family, neighbours and police.",
 "The humour comes from imagination running away with fear. The piece teaches us to look for simple causes before panicking, and it enjoys the funny side of family life and an eccentric grandfather.",
 "Words to learn: hullabaloo, burglar, supposed, suspected and absolute humour. Thurber writes in the first person with a dry, comic tone.",
 ["humour","hullabaloo","burglar","misunderstanding","narrator"],
 ("There really was a ghost.",["The family only imagined one.","The muddle came from misunderstandings."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this piece?|James Thurber|Ruskin Bond|Aesop|Thurber.","m|What did the narrator first think made the noise?|His father or brother Roy|A ghost|A dog|He supposed it was his family.","t|The story is humorous.|T|Imagination leads to humour.","m|Hullabaloo means|a noisy confusion|silence|a prize|A loud commotion.","s|On which date in November did it happen?|17;17th;17 november|17 November 1915.","t|The narrator is sure it is a ghost at once.|F|That thought came later."])
E(2,U2,"grumble-family","The Grumble Family (Lucy Maud Montgomery)",
 "Some people can find a fault in sunshine and in rain.",
 "The poem describes a family that lives on Complaining Street in the city of Never-Are-Satisfied, beside the River of Discontent. They growl about everything: the weather is always too hot or too cold, they scold in summer and winter, and nothing ever goes right for them.",
 "The poet uses humour and exaggeration to show how grumbling spoils a happy life. It invites us to mend our ways and be cheerful and thankful.",
 "Learn the word amiss, discontent and growl. Notice the invented place names that make the point, and the rhyming couplets.",
 ["grumble","discontent","couplet","exaggeration","complain"],
 ("The Grumble family is happy with everything.",["They complain about all things.","They live on Complaining Street."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Grumble Family?|Lucy Maud Montgomery|Frost|Kipling|L. M. Montgomery.","m|Where do the Grumbles live?|Complaining Street|Happy Lane|Dull Road|On Complaining Street.","t|They complain about the weather.|T|It is always too hot or cold.","m|What is the poem's lesson?|Do not grumble|Complain more|Move house|Be cheerful.","s|What is the river's name?|River of Discontent;Discontent|The River of Discontent.","t|The family is liked by everyone.|F|Nobody likes to meet them."])
E(2,U2,"zigzag","Zigzag: English Oddities",
 "Why is the plural of mouse mice but the plural of house not hice?",
 "This light-hearted poem plays with the odd rules of English. It points out irregular plurals such as mouse and mice, goose and geese, man and men, foot and feet, tooth and teeth, and asks why booth is not beeth or boot not beet.",
 "The poem teaches that English has many exceptions, so we should learn irregular forms carefully and enjoy the language's quirks. It also shows how rhyme and humour make grammar memorable.",
 "Grammar focus: irregular plurals. Regular plurals add s or es; irregular ones change the word or stay the same. Compare house and houses with mouse and mice.",
 ["irregular plural","oddities","rhyme","goose","geese"],
 ("All English plurals just add s.",["Some are irregular.","Mouse becomes mice."]),
 ["m|Plural of mouse is|mice|mouses|mices|An irregular plural.","m|Plural of goose is|geese|gooses|goosen|Geese.","t|The plural of house in the poem is hice.|F|It is houses.","m|Plural of tooth is|teeth|tooths|toothes|Teeth.","s|Plural of foot?|feet|Feet.","t|The poem is humorous.|T|It plays with odd rules."])
U3="Unit 3: Empowered Women Navigating the World, I am Every Woman, The Story of Mulan"
E(3,U3,"empowered-women","Empowered Women Navigating the World",
 "Six women set sail to go all the way around the globe.",
 "The text reports the Indian Navy's all-women crew, skippered by Lt. Cdr. Vartika Joshi, which broke many stereotypes by sailing around the world. The other crew members named are Lt. Cdr. Pratibha Jamwal, Lt. Cdr. Swathi Patarapalli, Lt. Aishwarya Boddapati, Lt. Vijaya Devi and Lt. Payal Gupta.",
 "The lesson compares this feat with earlier Indian circumnavigations, such as Capt. Dilip Donde's solo voyage in 2009-10 aboard INSV Mhadei. It shows that women are equal to men in courage and skill.",
 "Learn the words circumnavigate, skipper, crew, stereotype and navigate. This is a news-style report, so find the who, what, when and where.",
 ["circumnavigate","skipper","crew","stereotype","navigate"],
 ("The all-women crew was led by a man.",["Lt. Cdr. Vartika Joshi was the skipper.","The crew were all women."]),
 ["m|Who skippered the all-women crew?|Lt. Cdr. Vartika Joshi|Capt. Dilip Donde|Mulan|Vartika Joshi.","m|How many women were in the crew?|Six|Two|Ten|A crew of six women.","t|Capt. Dilip Donde made a solo circumnavigation.|T|From 2009 to 2010.","m|Circumnavigate means|sail around the world|climb|fly|Go all the way around.","s|Which ship did Capt. Donde sail?|INSV Mhadei;Mhadei|INSV Mhadei.","t|The crew broke stereotypes.|T|Yes."])
E(3,U3,"i-am-every-woman","I am Every Woman (Rakhi Nariani Shirke)",
 "A poem that praises the many roles a woman plays and her courage.",
 "The poet describes a woman as a symbol of power and strength. She puts her life at stake, is real and not fake, and carries her duties with care. She is ready for the summer of life even in spring and has no fear.",
 "The poem presents women as empowered, brave and resolute, and says that respect for women matters for a nation's growth.",
 "Notice the rhymes (strength and stake, fake and stake), the repetition of She, and words like innate, resolute and empowered.",
 ["empowered","resolute","innate","strength","respect"],
 ("The poem says women are weak.",["It says they are strong and fearless.","Women are empowered."]),
 ["m|Who wrote I am Every Woman?|Rakhi Nariani Shirke|Frost|Blake|Rakhi Nariani Shirke.","m|The woman is a symbol of|power and strength|weakness|fear|Power and strength.","t|The poem says she has no fear.|T|She is The Woman.","m|Resolute means|determined|lazy|afraid|Firm and determined.","s|The poem says respect for women helps the growth of the ...|nation|A nation.","t|The poem insults women.|F|It praises them."])
E(3,U3,"story-of-mulan","The Story of Mulan",
 "When her father is called to war, a girl takes his place.",
 "In ancient China the Emperor orders one man from each family to join the army. Mulan's father is ill and her brother is a child, so Mulan, a teenage girl from a faraway village, decides to go in his place.",
 "The tale is a legend based on Hua Mulan. A legend is a story from long ago believed to be true or mostly true. It shows a daughter's love, courage and wit, and that girls can do what is thought to be only for men.",
 "Words to learn: legend, emperor, army, brave and village. Compare a legend (about a real or supposed person) with a folk tale.",
 ["legend","Mulan","emperor","courage","army"],
 ("Mulan's brother went to war.",["He was a child.","Mulan took her father's place."]),
 ["m|Mulan lived in|a faraway village in China|Japan|Holland|China.","m|Why could her father not go?|He was unwell and old|He was lazy|He was king|He was not well.","t|Mulan took her father's place.|T|She went to war.","m|A legend is|a story from long ago believed mostly true|a modern novel|a poem|By definition.","s|Which country was in the middle of a great war?|China|China.","t|Her brother was a grown man.|F|He was a child."])
U4="Unit 4: The Attic, The Ant and the Cricket, The Aged Mother"
E(4,U4,"the-attic","The Attic (Satyajit Ray)",
 "A rich man visits his birthplace to put right something he did long ago.",
 "The narrator and his friend Aditya, a successful man building a new factory, stop at a fork on the highway. Aditya chooses to revisit Bramhapur, the town of his birth, and there takes out a packet and hands it to a gentleman, saying it is from Aditya. The jeweller they visit later says the article is an antique.",
 "Everyone makes mistakes, but a good person tries to rectify them. The story, told by Satyajit Ray, shows regret, honesty and the courage to repay a wrong. The attic was a favourite place for the children.",
 "Look for how the narrator slowly reveals the past through dialogue. Learn the words bifurcate, antique, affluent and rectify.",
 ["rectify","antique","affluent","attic","regret"],
 ("Aditya never made a mistake.",["He rectified a past wrong.","The story shows regret."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Attic?|Satyajit Ray|Ruskin Bond|Twain|Satyajit Ray.","m|Where was Aditya born?|Bramhapur|Chennai|Delhi|He revisits Bramhapur.","t|Aditya gave a packet to a gentleman.|T|It was from Aditya.","m|Antique means|very old and valuable|new|cheap|Old and valuable.","s|What does rectify mean?|put right;correct|To put right.","t|The story is about correcting a mistake.|T|Yes."],
 "(The exact details of the childhood incident are told in the story itself; read it in your book.)")
E(4,U4,"ant-and-the-cricket","The Ant and the Cricket (from Aesop's fables)",
 "One creature sang all summer; the other stored food for winter.",
 "A silly young cricket, used to singing through the warm months, finds his cupboard empty when winter comes. There is not a crumb on the snow-covered ground and no flower or leaf. Starving, he goes to the ant, who has worked and stored food, to ask for help.",
 "A fable is a traditional story that teaches a moral lesson, usually with animals as characters. This poem teaches the importance of hard work and planning for the future.",
 "Learn the words cupboard, famine, starvation, fable and moral. Note the poem's lively rhyme and speech marks.",
 ["fable","moral","hard work","planning","famine"],
 ("The cricket saved food for winter.",["He sang all summer.","The ant worked and saved."]),
 ["m|A fable teaches|a moral lesson|a recipe|a sum|It teaches a moral.","m|Who worked hard in summer?|The ant|The cricket|The bird|The ant.","t|The cricket was hungry in winter.|T|His cupboard was empty.","m|What is the moral?|Work and plan ahead|Sing always|Sleep|Hard work and planning.","s|Who composed the original fables?|Aesop|Aesop.","t|The poem is about two insects.|T|An ant and a cricket."])
E(4,U4,"aged-mother","The Aged Mother (Matsuo Basho)",
 "Wisdom does not fade with age.",
 "Long ago, a poor farmer lived at the foot of a mountain with his aged, widowed mother. In the country, the old were to be abandoned, and the farmer, with a heavy heart, prepared food for her, tied it in a square cloth and carried her up the mountain.",
 "This Japanese folk tale shows the love between a son and his mother and that the aged are sharp-witted and worthy of respect. The mother's wisdom later helps the people.",
 "Learn the words aged, widowed, humble and folktale. Notice the way the story begins with Long, long ago.",
 ["folktale","aged","widowed","wisdom","respect"],
 ("The old are always foolish.",["The mother was wise.","The tale respects the aged."]),
 ["m|Whom did the farmer live with?|His aged mother|His wife|His brother|His widowed mother.","m|Where did he carry her?|Up the mountain|To the sea|To school|Up the mountain.","t|The tale is Japanese.|T|It is a Japanese folktale.","m|The tale shows that the aged are|sharp-witted|useless|angry|Wise.","s|What did he carry food in?|cloth;a square cloth|A square cloth.","t|The farmer was rich.|F|He was poor."])
U5="Unit 5: Tech Bloomers, The Secret of the Machines, A Day in 2889"
E(5,U5,"tech-bloomers","Tech Bloomers",
 "Technology can give a disabled person freedom to travel, talk, study and work.",
 "The lesson tells how technology has changed the lives of Alisha and David. David controls an instrument with his eye movements, sends and receives text messages and plays MP3 music on his ECO2 device, and has been chosen to travel to Brazil with the Remix Drama Group for the Olympic opening ceremony.",
 "According to the 2011 Census, 2.21 percent of India's population is disabled, and attitudes are often the greatest barrier. Indian innovations such as Lechal shoes by Krispian Lawrence use GPS and Bluetooth to help the visually impaired navigate.",
 "Words to learn: disabled, assistive, innovation, navigate and barrier. This is an informative, report-style text with facts and examples.",
 ["technology","disability","innovation","Lechal shoes","barrier"],
 ("Technology cannot help disabled people.",["David and Alisha use it.","Lechal shoes guide users."]),
 ["m|What do Lechal shoes use?|GPS and Bluetooth|Magic|Wheels|They guide by GPS.","m|Who invented Lechal shoes?|Krispian Lawrence|Alisha|David|Krispian Lawrence.","t|David controls a device with his eyes.|T|By eye movements.","m|The 2011 Census figure for disabled people is|2.21 percent|50 percent|10 percent|2.21 percent.","s|Where was David chosen to travel?|Brazil|Brazil.","t|Technology can be used well or badly.|T|The way we use it decides."])
E(5,U5,"secret-of-the-machines","The Secret of the Machines (Rudyard Kipling)",
 "Machines tell us what they can do and who they really serve.",
 "The poem begins by telling how machines are made and how they should be treated. Then the machines speak and explain how they serve humanity: they can do great deeds and heavy work.",
 "The poem ends with the idea that the machines, however powerful, are still only creations of the human brain. It is a comment on technology and automation.",
 "Notice personification, because machines speak. Learn the words automation, deed and creation.",
 ["automation","personification","machines","creation","Kipling"],
 ("Machines rule humans according to the poem.",["They are made by humans.","They serve humanity."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this poem?|Rudyard Kipling|Frost|Blake|Rudyard Kipling.","m|Who speaks in the poem?|The machines|Animals|Kings|The machines.","t|The poem says machines are creations of the human brain.|T|Yes.","m|Personification means|giving human qualities to things|a metaphor of weather|a rhyme|Machines speak.","s|What do machines do for humanity?|serve;work|They serve humanity.","t|The poem is about modern technology.|T|Yes."])
E(5,U5,"day-in-2889","A Day in 2889 of an American Journalist",
 "How might a newspaper owner's day look a thousand years from now?",
 "This science-fiction story by Jules Verne imagines the year 2889 in a city called Centropolis, the new name of New York. An American newspaper owner lives with gadgets such as the phonotelephote, which carries both voice and picture, and phototelegrams, which send pictures by telegraph.",
 "The story predicts technologies that look surprisingly like today's video calls. It reminds us that science fiction imagines the future from present knowledge.",
 "Glossary words: phonotelephote, phototelegram, valet, Jovians and inhabitants. Learn how writers describe future inventions.",
 ["science fiction","phonotelephote","phototelegram","Centropolis","Jovians"],
 ("The story is set in the 19th century.",["It is set in 2889.","It imagines the future."]),
 ["m|The story is set in|2889|1889|1999|The year 2889.","m|Which device carries voice and picture?|Phonotelephote|Telegram|Radio|The phonotelephote.","t|Centropolis is the new name of New York.|T|In the story.","m|Who are Jovians?|Inhabitants of Jupiter|Americans|Indians|Jovians live on Jupiter.","s|Who wrote the story?|Jules Verne|Jules Verne.","t|It is a science-fiction story.|T|Yes."])
U6="Unit 6: The Last Lesson, No Men Are Foreign, The Little Hero of Holland"
E(6,U6,"the-last-lesson","The Last Lesson (Alphonse Daudet)",
 "A boy hurries to school expecting a scolding and finds a lesson he will never forget.",
 "The story is set in the days of the Franco-Prussian war, after which the districts of Alsace and Lorraine fell to the Prussians. Franz starts for school very late, fearing a scolding from M. Hamel, but finds the school unusually quiet and the villagers sitting at the back. He learns that this is the last French lesson because an order from Berlin says only German will be taught.",
 "Franz now regrets wasting time and not learning his language well. The story shows how a people's language is part of their identity and how we value things only when we are about to lose them.",
 "Learn the words dread, scolding, oppressors, identity and patriotism. The story is told in the first person by Franz.",
 ["language","identity","patriotism","Alsace","last lesson"],
 ("Franz was punished for being late.",["M. Hamel was kind that day.","It was the last French lesson."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Last Lesson?|Alphonse Daudet|Satyajit Ray|Doyle|Daudet.","m|Who is the teacher?|M. Hamel|Mr Smith|Prospero|M. Hamel.","t|It is the last French lesson.|T|Only German will be taught.","m|Alsace and Lorraine were taken by|Prussia|India|Spain|The Prussians.","s|Who is the narrator?|Franz|Franz.","t|Franz was eager to learn French at the start.|F|He was playful and late."])
E(6,U6,"no-men-are-foreign","No Men Are Foreign (James Falconer Kirkup)",
 "Beneath every uniform, a single body breathes like ours.",
 "The poet reminds us that no men are strange and no countries foreign. Beneath all uniforms a single body breathes like ours, the land others walk on is earth like ours, and they too are aware of sun, air and water.",
 "The poem says we must not hate people because they belong to another country or speak another language. All people are part of the brotherhood of men, and to fight against others is unnatural, a fight against ourselves.",
 "Notice the repeated idea of sameness and the use of we and our. Learn the words brethren, harvest and foreign.",
 ["brotherhood","foreign","uniform","harvest","unity"],
 ("The poem says other countries are our enemies.",["It says no men are foreign.","All are brothers."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this poem?|James Falconer Kirkup|Frost|Montgomery|Kirkup.","m|The poem stresses|universal brotherhood|war|trade|Brotherhood of men.","t|The poem says all men are alike beneath uniforms.|T|A single body breathes.","m|What lies in the earth for all men?|They all shall lie in the same earth|Gold|Fire|The same earth.","s|What do all people feel: sun, air and ...?|water|Water.","t|The poet supports war.|F|He opposes hatred."])
E(6,U6,"little-hero-of-holland","The Little Hero of Holland",
 "A hole no longer than a finger can threaten a whole village.",
 "Holland has much land below sea level, and great walls called dikes keep the North Sea out. Even little children know that a small hole in a dike is dangerous. In the story a young boy, Peter, finds a leak in the dike and stays there, plugging it, to save his people.",
 "It is a true-style story of a little boy with a brave heart and love for his village. It teaches courage, responsibility and that one person's brave act can save many.",
 "Words to learn: dike, leak, flood, hero and sea level. The story uses simple, clear sentences and builds suspense.",
 ["dike","leak","flood","hero","responsibility"],
 ("Peter ignored the hole and ran away.",["He stayed to plug it.","He saved the village."]),
 ["m|What keeps the North Sea out of Holland?|Dikes|Trees|Houses|Great walls called dikes.","m|Who is the little hero?|Peter|Franz|Aditya|Peter.","t|Much of Holland is below sea level.|T|Yes.","m|What does he do?|Plugs the leak|Plays|Sleeps|He blocks the hole.","s|Which sea threatens Holland?|North Sea|The North Sea.","t|A small hole can be dangerous.|T|Yes."])
U7="Unit 7: The Dying Detective, The House on Elm Street, A Dilemma"
E(7,U7,"dying-detective","The Dying Detective (Arthur Conan Doyle)",
 "Sherlock Holmes seems to be dying, and only Watson can be brought to his bedside.",
 "Mrs. Hudson, Holmes's landlady, tells Watson that Holmes has been sinking for three days and will not let a doctor come. Watson finds his friend weak and strange, and Holmes does not let him treat him. Holmes then sets a trap for the man who he believes caused the illness.",
 "The story teaches that appearances may deceive and that a clever mind uses cunning to catch a criminal. It shows Holmes's brilliant planning and Watson's loyalty.",
 "Learn the words landlady, sinking, deceive, trap and clue. Detective stories build suspense through hints and a final surprise.",
 ["detective","landlady","trap","suspense","deceive"],
 ("Holmes was really dying.",["He pretended to be ill.","It was a trap."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Dying Detective?|Arthur Conan Doyle|Satyajit Ray|Twain|Conan Doyle.","m|Who is Holmes's landlady?|Mrs. Hudson|Mrs. Watson|Aunt Polly|Mrs. Hudson.","t|Holmes would not let a doctor come.|T|He refused.","m|Who is Holmes's friend?|Watson|Hamel|Peter|Dr Watson.","s|Who told Watson that Holmes was dying?|Mrs. Hudson|Mrs. Hudson.","t|The story is a detective story.|T|Yes."])
E(7,U7,"house-on-elm-street","The House on Elm Street (Nadia Bush)",
 "A lonely house at the roadside keeps its secret.",
 "The poem describes a mysterious house that sits alone. Nobody knows what happened inside. At night the lights seem to flicker, and the speaker, who drives past almost every day, is tempted to look closer but fear takes over. Beside the house stands a tree that never grows leaves in any season.",
 "The poem builds mystery and fear through its images. Rumours grow and the house begins to fade, and the speaker never finds out the truth, which leaves the reader curious.",
 "Notice the irregular rhymes and the use of mood words like mysterious, flicker and rumours.",
 ["mystery","flicker","rumour","image","mood"],
 ("The speaker enters the house boldly.",["Fear takes over.","He only drives past."]),
 ["m|Who wrote the poem?|Nadia Bush|Frost|Kipling|Nadia Bush.","m|What is odd about the tree?|It never grows leaves|It is golden|It talks|It stays bare.","t|The speaker knows what happened in the house.|F|He does not know.","m|What does fear do to the speaker?|Stops him going in|Makes him brave|Makes him sing|He stays away.","s|On which street is the house?|Elm Street|Elm Street.","t|The poem creates mystery.|T|Yes."])
E(7,U7,"a-dilemma","A Dilemma (Silas Weir Mitchell)",
 "A man inherits a precious box and cannot rest because of it.",
 "The narrator is thirty-seven when his uncle Philip, a rich engineer and collector of rubies and pearls whom he has never met, dies. He is left a precious box that he fears might explode if shaken. He talks of it so much that library attendants think him a madman or a dynamite fiend and tell the police, and he is shadowed as a suspicious character.",
 "The story is a dilemma, a difficult choice, and it shows how fear and worry can take over a mind. It builds suspense with the narrator's anxious thoughts.",
 "Learn the words inventor, bachelor, mania, gems and dilemma. Note the first-person narration and the suspense.",
 ["dilemma","inheritance","suspense","gems","anxiety"],
 ("The narrator knew his uncle well.",["He had never seen him.","His uncle disliked his mother."]),
 ["m|Who wrote A Dilemma?|Silas Weir Mitchell|Doyle|Thurber|Silas Weir Mitchell.","m|Who was Uncle Philip?|A rich engineer and gem collector|A doctor|A farmer|An inventor.","t|The narrator had never seen his uncle.|T|Yes.","m|A dilemma is|a difficult choice|a gift|a song|A hard choice.","s|What did the uncle collect?|gems;precious stones;rubies and pearls|Precious stones.","t|The narrator worries about his box.|T|He fears it might explode."],
 "(The final outcome is revealed in the story; read it in your book.)")
